Write a self-contained prompt that instructs an AI coding agent to recreate a
Python script for Termux that dedupes/normalizes Python source by content hash,
walking archives and directories.
I'll describe what I can infer from the visible code, noting the code is
truncated.
Note: the code provided is truncated mid-function (in
`extract_with_tree_sitter`), so the prompt below covers the full behavior as far
as it is visible and frames the unseen parts as reasonable requirements. ---
Create a single, self-contained Python 3 command-line script intended to run
under Termux on Android (use the shebang
`#!/data/data/com.termux/files/usr/bin/env python`).
The script analyzes Python source code files, extracts top-level and nested
Python objects (functions, classes, and similar definitions), and groups
duplicate or near-duplicate code by content hash.
It must work on both loose files and inside archives. **Purpose and overall
behavior** - Scan a given input path (a directory or a single file, including
archives) for Python source files. - Read each `.py` source as UTF-8 text,
normalizing line endings (convert `\r\n` and lone `\r` to `\n`). - Parse the
source with tree-sitter (using the `tree_sitter_python` grammar and the
`tree_sitter.Language` / `Parser` API) to extract object definitions.
Use Python's `ast` module as a supporting or fallback mechanism where
appropriate. - Compute SHA-256 hashes of extracted object text (encode as UTF-8,
use hexdigest) and group identical hashes together, so that duplicate objects
across files are reported. - Optionally write results to output files.
**Dependencies** - Standard library: `argparse`, `ast`, `bz2`, `gzip`,
`hashlib`, `lzma`, `multiprocessing`, `sys`, `tarfile`, `tempfile`, `zipfile`,
`collections.defaultdict`, `pathlib.Path`. - Third-party: `brotli` (for `.br`
files), `tree_sitter_python`, `tree_sitter` (Language, Parser), `zstandard`
(imported as `zstd`, for `.zst`), and `loguru` for logging. - Include a
module-level flag `TREE_SITTER_AVAILABLE = True` indicating tree-sitter support.
**Supported archive formats** Recognize and handle these suffixes (order matters
for compound suffixes such as `.tar.gz`, `.tar.bz2`, `.tar.xz`): `.zip`, `.tar`,
`.tar.gz`, `.tgz`, `.tar.bz2`, `.tbz2`, `.tar.xz`, `.txz`, `.gz`, `.bz2`, `.xz`,
`.zst`, `.br`.
Store these in a module-level tuple named `SUPPORTED_ARCHIVES`. - Tarballs and
zip files should be iterated and their `.py` members read in memory or via a
temporary directory created with `tempfile`. - Single-file compressed formats
(`.gz`, `.bz2`, `.xz`, `.zst`, `.br`) contain one payload; decompress it and
treat it as a Python source file if its inner name ends in `.py`. **Helper
functions (required)** - `sha256(content: str) -> str`: returns hex SHA-256 of
UTF-8 encoded content. - `safe_read_text(path: Path) -> str | None`: reads UTF-8
text; on any exception logs an error via loguru and returns `None`. -
`safe_write_text(path: Path, content: str) -> bool`: writes UTF-8 text; on
exception logs and returns `False`; returns `True` on success. -
`normalize_newlines(s: str) -> str`: as described above. -
`extract_with_tree_sitter(code: str)`: builds a parser with the Python language
from `tree_sitter_python`, parses the code, walks the syntax tree, and collects
definitions (functions, classes, and their source text and line ranges) into a
list. **Error handling and edge cases** - Never crash on a single bad file: wrap
per-file processing in try/except, log with loguru, and continue. - Handle
unreadable files, decoding errors (non-UTF-8 content), corrupted archives, empty
files, and files with syntax errors (tree-sitter tolerates errors; AST parsing
should be wrapped in try/except `SyntaxError`). - Skip non-Python files and
directories that cannot be accessed. - Guard against archive path traversal
(member names containing `..` or absolute paths must not be written outside the
temp directory). - Treat empty objects or whitespace-only content as not worth
reporting. **Concurrency** - Use `multiprocessing` (a `Pool` or similar) to
process many files in parallel, with the worker count defaulting to
`os.cpu_count()` or a CLI option.
Ensure worker functions are module-level so they are picklable.
Protect the `__main__` entry with `if __name__ == "__main__":`. **Command-line
interface** - Use `argparse`.
Required: an input path.
Useful options: output file or directory, number of workers, minimum object
size/line count to report, a flag to show only duplicates, and a
verbosity/log-level option. - Print or write a report that lists each duplicate
group: the SHA-256 hash, the number of occurrences, and each source location
(file path, archive member name if applicable, and line range). - Exit with
status 0 on success and a non-zero status on fatal errors (e.g., input path does
not exist), using `sys.exit`. **Logging** - Use `loguru.logger` for all
diagnostics (info for progress summaries, error for per-file failures, warning
for skipped items).
Do not use bare `print` for diagnostics; reserve stdout for the report. **Code
quality expectations** - Use `from __future__ import annotations` and type hints
throughout. - Keep functions small and documented with brief docstrings. -
Produce a complete, runnable script with no placeholders; ensure every function
is fully implemented, including the tree-sitter traversal in
`extract_with_tree_sitter` and the main entry point. - Output only the script
itself when reproducing it. ---
